# Line endings are not cosmetic for the launchers — they decide whether
# the tool starts at all.
#
# cmd.exe requires CRLF. Given an LF-only .cmd it eats the first
# character of every line, so "SEO Tool.cmd" spews things like
# "'EO' is not recognized as an internal or external command".
# sh requires LF. Given a CRLF .sh it reports "bad interpreter: ...^M".
#
# Without these rules the endings came from whoever last checked the
# file out: a Windows clone (core.autocrlf=true) got working .cmd files,
# but the ZIP built on GitHub's Linux servers got LF — which broke the
# one-file launcher for exactly the non-technical users it exists for.
# Pinning eol here fixes clones and the release ZIP alike.

* text=auto

*.cmd      text eol=crlf
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.ps1      text eol=crlf
*.hta      text eol=crlf

*.sh       text eol=lf
*.command  text eol=lf
